    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters May 10 with a lineup optimized for high-ceiling performance, led by **Corbin Carroll** (71.12), **James Wood** (68.97), and **Trevor Story** (68.45). **Corbin Carroll** remains our premier play despite a slight penalty for the opponent\u2019s elite holding capability; his favorable platoon split against Brazob\u00e1n and prime #2 lineup positioning make him the catalyst for our category output. Similarly, **James Wood** enjoys an excellent matchup against Alcantara, benefiting from an elite +10% order bonus as the leadoff hitter and a strong 0.377 xwOBA, while **Trevor Story** thrives today due to a significant +13% SP skill advantage against Martinez, making him our most efficient middle-infielder despite the lower base projection.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved leaving **Cole Young** (43.57) on the bench. While **Cole Young** posted a respectable score buoyed by decent SP metrics, he was edged out by the superior depth and situational platoon advantages provided by the likes of **Zach McKinstry** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**. **Vinnie Pasquantino** in particular represents a massive efficiency play today, as his +14% dynamic platoon advantage against Hanifee elevates his daily utility score well into the strong territory (64.21). Meanwhile, we have opted to sit **Jose Caballero** entirely; his score of 39.32 falls below our 40.0 floor, heavily impacted by an unfavorable matchup against Henderson and poor platoon splits, ensuring we preserve our game caps for more productive profiles.\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the risks inherent in today's rotation. **Seiya Suzuki** presents a notable concern, as facing deGrom triggers a significant -19% SP skill penalty, dragging his daily score down to a marginal 44.29; he stays in the lineup only due to a lack of elite alternatives in the current OF pool. Furthermore, we are operating with \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Austin Riley**, **Zach McKinstry**, and **Vinnie Pasquantino**. Should any of these players drop further down their respective lineups, those -6% to -8% order penalties will compress their efficiency significantly, requiring close monitoring before the first pitch to ensure we aren't burning cap space on suboptimal batting positions.",
